# Routing only. No credential: neither registry requires or reads one, in either direction, and an
# install needs nothing at all — only `npm publish` has a client-side pre-flight, and nothing here
# publishes.
#
# TWO SERVICES since the byte-plane split. mirror.dev.localhost:8080 is the edge vhost for
# qits-platform-mirror, which holds the npmjs pull-through cache; registry.dev.localhost:8080 is
# qits-artifacts, which hosts the @qits scope. Both are the local platform's, and both exist for
# the one consumer this file is for: a developer on the deployment host, dialling them directly
# inside the trusted surface. Nothing else reads it — every build runs in a container on qits-net,
# where these addresses do not exist, and takes the qits-net aliases from the environment.
#
# CI overrides this file's effect rather than editing it: the pipeline sets npm_config_registry /
# npm_config_@qits:registry from $QITS_NPM_PROXY_URL / $QITS_NPM_REGISTRY_URL — the qits-net
# aliases (qits-platform-mirror:8080 and qits-artifacts:8080) — and environment outranks both a
# project and a user .npmrc.
#
# The lockfile is a separate problem, and it is npm's: npm records an absolute `resolved` URL per
# package, so package-lock.json spells these addresses ~700 times and is not host-neutral the way
# pnpm-lock.yaml was. .config/qits/ci-event-release-request.yml retargets those origins before
# `npm ci`, and the comment there explains why npm's own `replace-registry-host` cannot do it.
registry=http://mirror.dev.localhost:8080/npm/npmjs/
@qits:registry=http://registry.dev.localhost:8080/artifacts/npm/npm/
